WEBVTT

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:03.997
Advanced grouping introduces an important NULL question.

00:00:03.997 --> 00:00:10.122
Subtotal and grand-total rows often show NULL in grouping columns because that dimension has been rolled up.

00:00:10.122 --> 00:00:13.702
But source data can also contain genuine NULL values.

00:00:13.702 --> 00:00:16.867
GROUPING helps us distinguish those two situations.

00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:23.002
A grouping code tells us which expressions are participating in the current row and which have been summarized

00:00:23.002 --> 00:00:23.852
away.

00:00:23.852 --> 00:00:30.212
The exact code is less important than the principle: do not label every NULL with COALESCE before you know why

00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:36.797
it is NULL. In a production report, using GROUPING can help you label rows accurately as detail, subtotal,

00:00:36.797 --> 00:00:40.267
or grand total without confusing structural placeholders with missing data.

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:44.302
Advanced grouping produces several aggregation levels in one result.

00:00:44.302 --> 00:00:50.462
The challenge is interpretation: NULL may mean a subtotal placeholder rather than missing source data.

00:00:50.462 --> 00:00:56.772
Use GROUPING metadata or explicit labels so downstream users can distinguish detail groups, subtotals,

00:00:56.772 --> 00:00:57.909
and grand totals.
